<<~ ahu slot=`#/bearing`>>
! Layer Composition ~ the relation is part of the artwork

A layer may have no complete aesthetic read in isolation. It can exist to modify, reveal, interrupt, shade, register or protect another layer. The artistic invitation states the relationship; an optional artifact contract states enforceable geometry and channel requirements; the composition ledger records the actual operations and preserved sources.

This is a discipline-specific companion, not a universal default. It does not create an `art-layer()` procedure or require paintings, textiles, installations, photographs and performances to behave like pixel stacks.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/roles`>>
!! Layer roles are responsibilities, not style prescriptions

* `source-bearing` — original keyline, photographed evidence, or other material that must remain traceable; edit on a derived layer when source preservation is required.
* `underpainting` — color, atmosphere or support operating beneath another member of the image.
* `glaze` — a field modifying contrast, color or local luminosity without claiming a freestanding subject.
* `registration-plate` — a displaced or transformed copy of selected source features, where its misalignment may be deliberate.
* `mask/protection` — controls an operation's spatial reach; presence in a file stack does not mean it should render visibly.
* `attribution/typography` — exact letterforms or credits, sometimes requiring a separately verified text plate.
* `diagnostic/backup` — comparison, recovery or hidden evidence; must not accidentally contribute to the publication image.

The same visual asset can change role between operations; name scope and responsibility at the relevant step. Never treat all layers as individually required to look complete.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/composition-ledger`>>
!! Minimum composition ledger

```toml
[[component]]
handle = "color-field"
role = "underpainting"
source = "retained unchanged"
canvas = "aligned to a named base"
transformation = "specified separately; reproducible or documented"
alpha = "actual per-pixel transparency, not artistic translucency"
blend = "record actual blend operation and opacity"
visibility = "visible / hidden / diagnostic"
relationship = "adds atmosphere beneath the source, without replacing its linework"
```

For every composite distinguish: source pixel alpha, editor layer opacity, source-over/blend mathematics, stack order, offsets, masks, resampling, and output flattening. Different editors may display or round blends differently; independently computed previews are evidence for a comparison, not a guarantee of pixel-identical rendering everywhere.

An editable file should preserve layer source references and dimensions; a flattened export should never be the sole surviving source unless the operator explicitly accepts that loss.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/registration`>>
!! Registration and intervention

A source-derived plate needs an explicit correspondence map: source selection, transform, coordinates, masks and whether transformations happen before or after regional cleanup. Preserve deliberate registration errors that carry the artistic read; remove unintended orphan fragments only where the evidence supports removing them.

If several geometric regions need different treatment, record region-specific interventions instead of applying one universal global correction. Verify both technical coverage and situated visual coherence. A mathematically aligned edge can be the wrong artistic edge.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/reversibility`>>
!! Backups, checks, and release

* Preserve original pixels where the commission or rights require it. Name every transformed derivative; keep a hidden original or a separable source rather than overwriting.
* Record which source images and layers were changed, which were merely re-rendered, and which remain bit-identical.
* Validate alpha and clipping in actual composite context; apparent transparency alone is insufficient.
* Where feasible, deliver an editable multichannel/multilayer master plus a clearly named flattened publication export.
* Use before/after views at scales relevant to the work, not one mandatory zoom level.
* If flattening or destructive editing is unavoidable, record the boundary and obtain the operator's acceptance.

Related: [[Artifact Contract|ha.ka.ba/lares/api/art-sigils/artifact-contract]] · [[Reception QA|ha.ka.ba/lares/api/art-sigils/reception-qa]] · [[Provenance and Publication|ha.ka.ba/lares/api/art-sigils/provenance-publication]].
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/aftermath`>>
The crucial relation is not `image A` plus `image B`, but the work performed by their encounter. Composition deserves its own craft discipline without colonizing the semantic nucleus.
<<~/ahu>>

